{"kind":"report","text":"Slice 9 ruling: reuse of the checked Rules genesis in one locate call (answers request 3b0ded5a; applies ruling b9e821dd).\n\n1. Accepted. Inside one archive-target.locate invocation, the second Rules birth read can reuse the result of the first checked birth read. The condition is exact equality of the full ScopeRef: scope, incarnation and kind.\n\n2. Reason. A genesis is immutable for a given full ScopeRef. A second read of it gives no new fact about the genesis. It only observes availability and trusted configuration a second time.\n\n3. Availability. The peer availability contract applies to each read that the pass makes. It does not require a second read only to observe availability again. A read that the pass makes and that fails stops the pass, and a later pass tries again. A peer failure after the first successful read does not make the checked result wrong.\n\n4. Configuration. Under b9e821dd, trusted configuration is fixed for the process. A catalog or support replacement between the two reads is a trusted-code defect. The checks at the consumption fence stay mandatory and unchanged: the final Rules pin, creatorDirectory, select and full-ref coherence, the Directory, Destination, two-item, binding and repository authority checks.\n\n5. Limits. The first actual birth read stays mandatory. An existing pin never replaces it. A different full ScopeRef keeps the original read. No cache across invocations, no reuse of a live policy admission, and no change to persistence. Charge the added equality work. Remove only the duplicate operation.\n\n6. Record. Name the omitted second observation as an accepted difference in the slice 9 implementation note. Keep witnesses for: equal full ScopeRef reuses and the bookkeeping delta is exact; a different full ScopeRef reads again; a failure at a fence check still refuses. The v16 and v32 failures stay recorded as natural failures. Claim a fit only from a run at the reviewed head.","body":{"to":"builder"}}